\subsection{Refinement strategy}
\label{sec:refinement}

We first run our code with FIRE physics up until the first star particle forms.
We then take the latest snapshot without this star particle, and trace this particle's
location in this snapshot, and tag particles with 
$r < r_\text{init} = $ %TODO: Value of initial refinement radius
of it. Center of mass of the tagged particles will be the center of the refinement
region.

%TODO: Refinement evolution

\subsection{Spatial cutouts}
\label{sec:cutouts}

The full simulation snapshot contains $\sim 88$~million CDM particles
and FIRE-resolution gas particles whose smoothing lengths
($\sim\!$kpc) far exceed the protostellar disk scale.
We extract spatial cutouts centred on the most-massive sink particle with
a comoving radius of $0.005\kpc/h \approx 330\AU$ (physical, at this epoch),
retaining only the high-resolution Jeans-refined gas
and sink/star particles.

%TODO

\subsection{Disk-identification algorithm}
\label{sec:diskid}

For calculating velocity dispersion, local Toomre Q, properties of the disk, we
identify particles in the disk for each snapshot.
This is done following the procedure below, and disk properties are obtained from
this subset of particles:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item The disk centre is placed at the most massive sink
    particle. This is more stable than the mass-weighted centre-of-mass (COM)
    of all sinks once secondary sinks form at large separations.
  \item A mass-weighted COM velocity
    $v_\mathrm{COM}$ is computed from gas particles within $r < r_\mathrm{search}
    = 10^{-5}\kpc \approx 2060\AU$ and subtracted from all particle velocities.
  \item The disk normal $\Lhat$ is computed from the
    total angular momentum $\mathbf{L} = \sum_i m_i\,(\mathbf{r}_i \times \mathbf{v}_i)$
    summed over particles within $r_\mathrm{search}$.
  \item Particle positions and velocities
    are projected onto the disk-plane coordinate system $(r_\mathrm{cyl},\,z,\,v_\phi,\,v_r,\,v_z)$.
  \item $r_\mathrm{cyl} < r_\mathrm{max} = 10^{-5}\kpc$,
    $|z|/r_\mathrm{cyl} < 0.3$ (aspect ratio), and $\rho > \rho_\mathrm{thresh}
    = 10^{-15}\gcm$.
  \item $v_\phi > 0$ (co-rotating) and
    $v_\phi / v_K > f_\mathrm{kep} = 0.3$, where
    $v_K = \sqrt{G M_\mathrm{enc}(<r)/r}$ is the Keplerian speed computed from
    the enclosed disk-gas plus sink mass.
\end{enumerate}
