\subsection{Toomre $Q$ space--time evolution}
\label{sec:toomre}

We plot evolution of radial profile of local Toomre Q on fig. \ref{figure:TODO}.

It can be seen that the disk (predictably) starts in the simulation in mostly 
unstable, prone to star formation state of $Q>1$.

The first sink predictably forms in the center of the disk, and proceeds
to accreete infalling gas. After around 1 kyr, we have a 2nd sink forming, still
in the center, and the behavior doesn't change significantly. 
It is likely that the new sink merges with the 1st sink shortly. %TODO: CHECK!

Initial collapse of the 1st and 2nd sinks is accompanied by the wave of
"stability" (i.e. "lowered" Toomre Q) propagating outwards at the speed ...  %TODO. soundspeed fraction?  
The cause for this stability comes from velocity
dispersion: i.e. we can see these waves propagating outward on the velocity
dispersion plots on fig.  \ref{figure:TODO}, but not on density projection
plots like fig.  \ref{figure:TODO}. This means that formation of individual
sinks results in outward-propagating shocks.

